  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c1bff6658b6d4fe18488eccfca7256bb, type: 3}
  m_Name: ManualPage3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  pageIndex: 2
  pageTitle: Flags
  leftPageBody: "TCP Flags:\r\nTCP flags are control bits that describe the purpose
    of a packet in the connection lifecycle. Common flags include SYN (start a connection),
    ACK (acknowledge receipt), FIN (close a connection), and RST (reset/abort a connection).
    These flags must follow a logical order\u2014for example, a connection normally
    starts with SYN and ends with FIN. Flags that appear out of sequence or in impossible
    combinations can indicate suspicious or malformed packets."
  rightPageBody: 'UDP Flags:

    The UDP protocol does not carry any flags in the
    payload. If you see any flags in a UDP packet, that would be highly suspicious.'
  requireSecondPageUnlock: 1
